Five strict inventories authenticate before/after: accepted search126, UI-negative166, revision-negative26, correction119, independent74. Preserve all516 path records (511 inventory entries plus5 exact original manifest objects), represented by174 unique public inert byte objects.73 can reuse authenticated already-installed path-correction history snapshots;101 new blobs suffice. The map retains original path→digest→public object mappings, full manifests, complete source/test/helper versions, stdout/stderr/observations and earlier failure/control classification. Repeated sources must not be recursively recopied into nested evidence trees. Use .snapshot for historical executable/Markdown files so they are not active tests/docs.

The UI-negative seven cases had4pass/3fail. Successful held responses followed a real correct disk Save, then lost a newer in-memory draft or updated a disposed view. The held error control was synthetic and had no disk Save/commit. The separate revision-negative three cases had2pass/1fail: a direct in-process caller could mutate input.revision across queued awaits and bypass the originally stale precondition; changing input.path/content did not alter their already captured values. This is not HTTP request object sharing or proof of an HTTP exploit.

