## Payload and scope exclusions

Selected516 historical path records map to174 unique plain source/raw/synthetic fixture objects. Finite first-header and quoted-base64 checks found0 native archive/executable/container carriers and0 credential-like JSON literal values. Only email-like literal was fixtures@example.invalid, an owned Git identity fixture.10 unique byte objects retain local Windows username/path metadata. These are explicit provenance/privacy metadata; this scan is not universal proof of no personal data or hidden secrets. No user credential/data files or vendor executable/archive bytes were selected. Original release archives/runtime acquisition remain in the prior separately preserved local epoch.

Preserve the correction's output metadata clarification: its initially named combined limit is actually4MiB maxBuffer per stream, with no threshold/runtime change. This plan's initial collector failed because it looked for groups on the focused receipt rather than the final receipt; its exact v1 source/raw remain, and v2 authenticated the identical already-written adapter bytes. Source-only path inspection mistakes are not candidate runtime failures. Independent review's controller uses60seconds/2MiB per captured stream and one controller plus three per-file workers; no universal descendant isolation or blocked-I/O preemption is claimed.

Keep all original159 source fingerprints and232 capability IDs unchanged. A finite Save publication/revision-precondition reliability supplement can be appended to existing editor local scope without declaring original Save-workbench parity or closing any completion gate. Current installed search receipts stay historical and unchanged; root adds a distinct current Save milestone only after actual verification. In-memory drafts remain lost on reload, closing a tab cannot undo an already committed disk write, external filesystem modification races remain open, and complete editing/extension/encoding/autosave/security/performance/product parity and superiority remain unestablished.
